<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephanie of Milly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1145–1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephanie of Milly was heiress of the lordship of Oultrejordain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-milly">Philip of Milly</a>. Her marriages carried the lordship to Humphrey III of Toron, then to Miles of Plancy and finally, in 1176, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>. After Raynald's death she sought the release of her captive son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a> by offering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> the castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephanie was one of three children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-milly">Philip of Milly</a> and his wife, Isabella, and was named after her Flemish paternal grandmother. Philip held wide lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, which in 1161 he exchanged for the Lordship of Transjordan, centred on the strongholds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a>. When he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Order of the Temple</a> in 1166, the lordship passed to Stephanie's sister, Helena, and on Helena's death to Helena's daughter, Beatrice. Stephanie inherited it only when Beatrice too had died, by February 1174.</p><p>Her marriage to Humphrey III of Toron, son of the constable Humphrey II, produced two children, Humphrey IV and Isabella. King Amalric then married her to his seneschal and favourite, Miles of Plancy, probably between 24 March and 18 April 1174. After Amalric's death Miles governed in the name of the young Baldwin IV, but he was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in October 1174 in a dispute with Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>, whom Stephanie believed responsible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Transjordan lay across the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, just as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> was extending his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which made Stephanie the most important heiress of the kingdom. Backed by her father-in-law and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, she stayed a widow for some time and ruled alone. Her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>, who had returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> early in 1176, became a serious threat to Saladin, and the couple sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> in the quarrels among the nobility.</p><p>In 1180 Humphrey was engaged to Isabella, half-sister of King Baldwin, and the girl was sent to live at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a>. During the wedding in October 1183 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged the castle. Ernoul, probably informed by the bride's mother, Queen Maria Komnene, tells that Stephanie sent dishes from the feast to Saladin, who then asked which tower the couple occupied so as to spare it. King Baldwin relieved the castle in early December.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187 she held full authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Imad al-Din al-Isfahani, who called her virtuous and intelligent, describes her agreeing in tears to give up the castles. The garrisons refused her order to surrender and accused her of siding with the Muslims, so she handed Humphrey back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Saladin, impressed, released him after a few months. Kerak held out until November 1188.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephanie of Milly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It passed to Philip of Milly and then, through the heiress <b>Stephanie of Milly</b>, to Raynald of Chatillon in 1176.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-oultrejordain">Lordship of Oultrejordain</a></li></ul></section>
